app.*.symbols

# Obfuscation related
app.*.map.json

# Android Studio will place build artifacts here
/android/app/debug
/android/app/profile
/android/app/release

# Native build output. `/build/` above only covers the repo root, so Gradle's
# and Xcode's own build directories were never ignored: five files under
# ios/build/XCBuildData were committed, and the dev branch added a 663-line
# android/build Gradle problems report. Build output is not source.
/android/build/
# Kotlin compiler session files. Gradle writes these during a build and they
# have no business in git — one was committed by a blanket `git add -A` after
# an APK build.
/android/.kotlin/
/android/*/build/
/ios/build/
/macos/build/

# CocoaPods working state — regenerated by `pod install`, never committed.
# (Podfile and Podfile.lock ARE tracked; these are not.)
/ios/Pods/
/ios/.symlinks/
/macos/Pods/
/macos/.symlinks/

# Generated by `flutter pub get`; contains absolute machine paths, same class
# of problem as android/local.properties above.
/ios/Flutter/Generated.xcconfig
/ios/Flutter/flutter_export_environment.sh
/ios/Flutter/ephemeral/

# Local machine config — never commit Windows/Mac absolute paths
/android/local.properties
/android/key.properties

# Release signing material. `/android/key.properties` above is the path Gradle
# actually reads — `rootProject` in android/app/build.gradle is the android/
# directory — but the root-level spelling was NOT matched, and a Play upload key
# committed once is a key that has to be treated as compromised. Play upload-key
# mistakes are close to unrecoverable, and this project has already been through
# re-signing pain requiring both certificates.
/key.properties
key.properties
*.jks
*.keystore
!android/app/debug.keystore

# R8 mapping. Not a secret, but it deobfuscates a shipped bundle, so it is
# uploaded to Crashlytics rather than committed.
/android/app/mapping.txt
mapping.txt

# Firebase CLIENT config is NOT a secret and IS committed, deliberately.
#
# These three files identify the Firebase project; they authorise nothing. Every
# one of them is extractable from any shipped APK or IPA in about a minute, which
# is why Google documents them as public. What protects the project is Firebase
# Security Rules and the backend's verifyIdToken — not the obscurity of an app id.
#
# They were untracked in 49acba9 as part of a credential sweep. That was wrong,
# and it broke the build for everyone: lib/main.dart imports firebase_options.dart,
# so CI could not analyze or build the app at all. The local checkout still had
# the file, so it looked fine on a developer machine and failed only in CI.
#
# The genuinely secret Firebase credential is the ADMIN SDK service account key,
# which lives in servana_api and is correctly untracked there.
#
# The three commented-out ignore lines that used to sit here are deleted rather
# than left as decoration (TAB 18). A commented ignore rule states no policy and
# does nothing, but it reads as an unfinished intention — and the accompanying
# note above it claimed the files were untracked when all three are, in fact,
# tracked. Two contradictory policies in one file is how the next credential
# sweep untracks them again and breaks the build a second time.
#
# The policy, stated once: the three Firebase CLIENT config files ARE tracked,
# deliberately, and there is no rule here to change that.
#
# What genuinely must never be committed is the Firebase ADMIN SDK service
# account key, which lives in servana_api and is correctly untracked there.
# Verified 2026-08-18: no file in this repository contains a `private_key`.

# Test coverage output — regenerated by `flutter test --coverage`
coverage/

# Claude Code personal overrides (machine-local, never shared)
.claude/settings.local.json
